## Runtime dependencies

This repository's packages declare the following as dependencies but **do not
vendor, bundle, or modify** any of them. They are installed by the consumer and
remain under their own licences:

| Package | Role | Declared as |
|---|---|---|
| `@typesafe-ai/sdk` | Official TypeSafe client, used by both the `live` and `openrouter` providers | optional dependency of `jevcore` |
| `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` | MCP server and stdio transport | dependency of `jevcore-mcp` |
| `zod` | Wire-schema validation for the MCP surface | dependency of `jevcore-mcp` |
| `@deepseek-ai/cordis` | Cordis service registry and plugin loader | optional peer dependency of `jevcore-dsh`, supplied by the harness at runtime |
| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools` | DeepSeek Harness tool registry | optional peer dependency of `jevcore-dsh`, supplied by the harness at runtime |

`@typesafe-ai/sdk` is the only optional dependency. It is loaded lazily, so an
install that omits it still runs — on the offline `mock` provider, which makes no
network call.

There is one vendor client, not two. The `openrouter` provider runs that same
official client against a different `baseURL`, because OpenRouter serves the
documented `/v1/systemone` endpoint. `@openrouter/sdk` was a second client for a
second, alpha-named payload shape; it was removed in **0.2.0** — the published
`0.1.2` metadata still lists it as `@openrouter/sdk@^1.3.6`, and every version
from `0.2.0` on lists only `@typesafe-ai/sdk`.

## Development dependencies

`typescript`, `vitest`, and `@types/node` are used to build and test this
project. They are not redistributed in the published packages.

## Vendor schemas

`packages/core/test/vendor-conformance.test.ts` **imports type declarations
only** from `@typesafe-ai/sdk` in order to check this project's question and
answer shapes against the vendor's own types, so that a drift in either
direction is a compile error rather than a runtime surprise. Those declarations
are the vendor's work, they are read from the installed package at build and
test time, and they are not copied into this repository.

The OpenRouter route has no separate schema to conform to: it is the same client
and the same request body against a different host, and the test asserts exactly
that. (The file `packages/core/scripts/check-openrouter-schemas.mjs`, which
parsed payloads against the removed client's schemas, no longer exists.)

